@$VER: llm_coding_cli.guide 1.1 (18.11.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Coding CLI as an LLM"
@{h1}Coding CLI as an LLM

A coding CLI -- Claude Code or Antigravity -- answers an agent's model call.
The agent runs as always: its prompt, its history, the hooks that add to it,
its session and its cancel. Only the call to the model goes elsewhere: to a
@{tt}coding_cli@{utt} instance, which runs the CLI on the operator's
subscription. The CLI reads the conversation as one task, runs its own tool
loop -- with the agent's own tools, handed to it through the MCP gateway --
and its final answer becomes the agent's assistant message.

Ready-made profiles ship with the plugin: pick @{tt}claude-code-sonnet@{utt}
or @{tt}agy-gemini-flash@{utt} in the chat's profile selector and the agent
you chat with runs on it.

@{code}
For everyone
  @{"  Running an agent on a CLI  " link use}
For developers
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  What one call does  " link call}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  When the next link answers  " link fallback}
  @{"  Known gaps  " link gaps}
@{body}
@endnode

@node use "Running an agent on a CLI"
@{h1}Running an agent on a CLI

@{h2}The profiles

@{table}
Profile | Runs on | Tools
claude-code-opus, claude-code-sonnet, claude-code-haiku | Claude Code, the operator's subscription | the agent's own
agy-gemini-flash, agy-gemini-hydrogen, agy-gemini-argon, agy-gemini-carbon, agy-gemini-neon | Antigravity (agy), the operator's Google login | the agent's own, beside agy's web search
@{body}

Pick one where any profile is picked: the chat's profile selector, @{tt}/model
<profile>@{utt}, @{tt}agent-cli run --llm <profile>@{utt}, or an agent's
@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} chain (first, with an API profile after it: the chain
moves on when the CLI cannot answer).

@{h2}What it needs

@{bullet}The CLI installed and logged in for the account ScarabHive runs
under (@{tt}claude@{utt}, @{tt}agy@{utt}); without it the profile fails at
once, and says so.
@{bullet}Your account in the instance's @{tt}allowed_users@{utt}
(@{tt}admin@{utt} and @{tt}cli_user@{utt} as shipped): the subscription
belongs to one person.
@{bullet}The agent running in the API process, or in one-shot
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt}: the instance runs where the agent runs.

@{h2}What is different from an API model

@{bullet}One model call is one CLI run: the CLI calls tools and goes on by
itself until it has the answer. The chat shows the run as a box with its
steps; the agent's tool calls appear as usual.
@{bullet}The tools Claude Code calls are the agent's own, with the agent's
rights: its allowlist, its approval questions, its files. They are not kept
in the agent's history -- the answer is.
@{bullet}agy takes about 55,000 tokens of a conversation; the profiles'
context window is set to that, so an agent with context management compacts
before a call would be refused. A longer conversation is refused with a
message that says so.
@{bullet}No streaming: the answer arrives when the run ends.
@{bullet}Costs: none per call -- the subscription pays. Its windows bound
Claude Code: at 80 % of a window no run starts until it resets.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

@{h2}What ships

@{tt}agents/llm_coding_cli.yaml@{utt} holds two coding_cli instances that only
answer model calls, the gateway their tools come through, and a model entry
and profile per model:

@{table}
Entry | What it is
claude_code_llm | coding_cli, @{tt}cli: claude@{utt}; one workdir without files (a room: no worktree, no file tools of Claude Code's own) whose gateway says @{tt}caller@{utt}; @{tt}models: [opus, sonnet, haiku]@{utt}
agy_llm | coding_cli, @{tt}cli: antigravity@{utt}; one web workdir (agy searches the web in every run) without a gateway; its @{tt}models@{utt} are ids of @{tt}agy models@{utt}
mcp_gateway_llm | mcp_gateway without a runner of its own: every grant is run by the agent that made the call
@{body}

Change them in your own configuration (the same keys under
@{tt}plugins.servers@{utt}), not in the plugin's file.

@{h2}The model entry

The provider is @{tt}claude_code@{utt} or @{tt}antigravity@{utt};
@{tt}model@{utt} names the coding_cli instance, alone or as
@{tt}<instance>/<model>@{utt} for a model of the CLI's the instance lists
(its @{tt}models@{utt}). @{tt}thinking_level@{utt} -- the entry's, or the
level picked for a chat -- is the run's effort (minimal counts as low, none
asks for none); for an agy model whose id names its level it is dropped.
@{tt}context_window@{utt} is what the agent's context management keeps the
conversation under, not something the CLI is sent: for agy set it to what the
instance takes whole (55000). A dialect key set here is named in the log as
ignored.

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    my-claude:
      provider: claude_code
      model: claude_code_llm/opus     # <coding_cli instance>/<its model>
      context_window: 200000
  profiles:
    on_subscription:
      model_ref: my-claude
@{body}

@{h2}The instance

@{bullet}Its @{tt}cli@{utt} matches the provider (@{tt}claude@{utt} for
claude_code, @{tt}antigravity@{utt} for antigravity), or every call falls
back with an error in the log.
@{bullet}It has exactly one workdir: the call names none.
@{bullet}The workdir's @{tt}gateway@{utt} decides the tools. @{tt}caller:
true@{utt} hands the CLI the tools of the agent it answers for: the call
names the tools the agent would have sent an API model, and the run reaches
exactly those, run by that agent -- its allowlist, its hooks, its session
(coding_cli guide, "Tools of this installation"). A gateway with a fixed
@{tt}tools@{utt} list and a runner of its own gives every agent the same
tools instead; without a gateway the CLI only answers.
@{bullet}A web workdir (agy: every one) gets a @{tt}caller@{utt} gateway only
with @{tt}with_web: true@{utt}: a page the run reads could steer the agent's
tools, and the operator says whether that is acceptable. The shipped agy
instance says yes, as for an agent with a web tool beside its others;
without its gateway agy only answers and searches.
@{bullet}The agent run's tool bindings (@{tt}Agent.run_events(tool_bindings=...)@{utt},
a sub-agent's spawn) go to the run with its call, so the CLI's tool calls are
held to the same values as the agent's own; a run without bindings sends
none, and a workdir without a gateway -- no tool of the agent's to hold --
passes them over. A workdir whose gateway requires values (@{tt}pin@{utt})
refuses a run that does not bring them, and the chain falls back.
@{bullet}The calling account is in its @{tt}allowed_users@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}max_task_chars@{utt} holds the whole conversation: the task is the
system prompt and the history as text, and coding_cli refuses a longer one
(20 000 characters unless set; the shipped instances take 1 000 000) -- every
call then falls back.
@{bullet}@{tt}max_result_chars@{utt} holds the longest final answer the agent
needs: a cut answer falls back.

The instance runs where the agent runs; an agent running elsewhere finds none
and falls back.
@endnode

@node call "What one call does"
@{h1}What one call does

@{bullet}It needs the agent run around it: its request id, user and session
(the session as @{tt}current_run_session@{utt}, set by every agent run). The
CLI's tool calls run in that session -- a store's documents are the agent's
own, as with its own calls. Without them the call falls back.
@{bullet}The first call of an agent run starts a run:
@{tt}<instance>_run_task@{utt} with the conversation as the task, @{tt}wake:
false@{utt}, the account, the session and the request id (the run's grant
and status are filed under it), the names of the call's tools
(@{tt}_tools@{utt}: what a @{tt}caller@{utt} workdir hands the run), and the
entry's model and effort where it names them. While the run goes on,
@{tt}<instance>_get_run@{utt} waits for it, 600 seconds at a time, with
@{tt}stop_if_cancelled@{utt}.
@{bullet}The run is the call: it ends with it. Its view in the chat is live
only and shows "model call of <agent>" as its task -- coding_cli keeps no
background run for it (@{tt}_background: false@{utt}), which would leave a
sub-session per model call, the whole conversation in each.
@{bullet}A later call of the same session -- a nudge or a format correction
in the same agent run, the next turn of a continued sub-agent (a new agent run,
built anew) -- resumes the run it goes on from with only the messages added
since: when the conversation it gets is one it gave before, grown (the longest
such one). The CLI's own answer, as the agent kept it, is not sent again. A
history compacted in between, another conversation (a hook's own model call),
another user or session, another model or effort than the run was asked for
(a chat switched to another profile or thinking level), or nothing new starts
a run of its own; so does a run
coding_cli will not resume (its answer's @{tt}code: resume_refused@{utt}: its
worktree busy or gone, other bindings) -- with the whole conversation, not a
move down the chain. The runs a session may go on with are kept in this
process: after a restart, and in every one-shot @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} turn, the
next call starts afresh.
@{bullet}Cancelling the agent cancels the wait, and coding_cli stops a run
whose waiting caller is cancelled; a wait that fails for another reason
stops the run too. A run whose agent's process has ended (a restart) is
stopped by whoever takes it over or watches it: nobody reads its answer.
@{bullet}The answer: the run's final text as the assistant message, no tool
calls, @{tt}served_by@{utt} @{tt}<provider>:<instance>@{utt}. Usage reports
cost 0 -- the subscription pays -- and the run's tokens: prompt tokens with
the cache's reads and writes among them, and the output, summed by the CLI
over its own model calls -- one run is one call, however many turns the CLI
took inside it. Claude Code's counts are read as Anthropic's API names them
(its @{tt}input_tokens@{utt} holds only what neither came from nor went into
the cache); agy counts its cache reads beside its input as well
(@{tt}cache_read_tokens@{utt}), its thinking inside its output. Being a sum, they are
not how full the agent's context is: the usage says
@{tt}reflects_context: false@{utt}, the usage store keeps the call as one
without a conversation (in its totals and cache share, never as the context
reading), and the chat's footer shows no fill for it. A run that ended
without a result (stopped, killed) reports none: its stream carries no
output count to sum. The message debugger sees the call through the
same notices as any client: the task as the payload, the run's answer as the
response, the CLI's model as the model.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

The CLI gets one task text, built from the messages in their order:

@{code}
# Instructions

<the system prompt>

## user

<a user message, hook insertions included>

## assistant

<an earlier answer>
[called <tool> with <arguments>]

## tool

<a tool result>
@{body}

The agent's tool schemas are not part of the text. With a @{tt}caller@{utt}
gateway the CLI finds the call's tools as MCP tools of the server
@{tt}gateway@{utt}, under the names the agent's prompt uses, prefixed by the
CLI: @{tt}mcp__gateway__<tool>@{utt} in Claude Code,
@{tt}mcp_gateway_<tool>@{utt} in agy, each with its description and
parameters. Without one it has the tools of its workdir's fixed gateway, or
none.
A part of a message that is not text (an image) is named, not passed:
@{tt}[image_url not passed on]@{utt}. A resumed run gets only the blocks of
the messages added since its last call.

@{h2}Token and cache effect

None on the agent's own requests: nothing is added to its prompt or history.
The CLI's own context is the CLI's. The runs of an instance without files
share one room, so the part every conversation of an agent starts with (the
system prompt) is read from the subscription's cache.
@endnode

@node fallback "When the next link answers"
@{h1}When the next link answers

@{table}
Case | Raised | The chain
the subscription's limit (coding_cli's limit field) | LLMQuotaExhaustedError | blocks this link until the limit resets (when coding_cli knows it), then moves on
not started for another reason (runs still going, git, the gateway) | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
the conversation is longer than the instance takes (max_task_chars; agy: max_task_tokens) | LLMServerError | moves on, no block -- its message says the size and the bound
a model the instance does not list | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
an agy run that failed on its login before its first turn | LLMServerError, if it fails again | starts once more after 15 s first; failed again, moves on, and for ten minutes no run of that user on that instance starts twice (a run that answers ends that)
an agy run that ended without an answer on a refused step (a command, say: agy then ends its whole conversation) | LLMServerError, if it ends so again | resumed once, told that such steps are refused and to go on with its other tools (measured for a command: it answers then); a second such end moves on. The call's usage holds both runs
a Claude Code run that did not reach its API (api_error connection_failed; measured: DNS gone, ENOTFOUND after about 3 min) | LLMConnectionError, caused by the error its code names | moves on; when no link of the step's chain reached a provider and one found no route, the step waits for the network (network_outage_seconds) -- a refused connection (ECONNREFUSED) is no outage
a run that failed, was stopped or was cancelled | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
an answer cut by max_result_chars, or none | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
the instance missing, or another cli than the provider's | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
no agent run around the call | LLMServerError | moves on, no block
@{body}

A profile picked for a chat is a chain of one: what would move a chain on is
the chat's error, in these words.

A failed run without tokens says nothing of the network (agy with the network gone fails at once on an
unknown model): it does not count as a provider that got the request, so a link before or after it decides
whether the step waits for the network. A run that counted tokens, an answer cut or empty, did reach its model --
so did one stopped before its result (its time limit), which counts none, when its stream shows the model
answering (coding_cli's @{tt}model_answered@{utt}): asked again after a wait, its work would run twice.

Each of them logs one line naming the provider, the instance and why; a
mismatched cli logs it as an error. Antigravity reports no subscription
windows: a run it stops at its limit is expected to arrive as a failed run
(not measured yet), which moves the chain on without a block.

What the client reads of a refusal is coding_cli's @{tt}code@{utt}
(@{tt}resume_refused@{utt}) and its @{tt}limit@{utt} field, never its words.
@endnode

@node gaps "Known gaps"
@{h1}Known gaps

@{bullet}The CLI's tool calls are the agent's, but not in the agent's loop:
they are not in its history, its step budget and loop detection do not count
them, and a long tool result is the CLI's to carry, not the agent's context
management's. The run's time limit and the subscription bound them.
@{bullet}A tool the gateway cannot run is missing for the CLI: tools of
external MCP servers, and whatever the agent's allowlist leaves out. A tool
result over the gateway's @{tt}max_result_chars@{utt} (60 000) is refused
with a note to ask for less; agy shows its model only the beginning and end
of a result over about 10 000 characters (the whole is in a file agy names).
@{bullet}agy searches the web in every run, and a page it reads could steer
the agent's tools: the shipped instance accepts that (@{tt}with_web@{utt});
an operator who does not removes its gateway.
@{bullet}A full instance (@{tt}max_parallel@{utt}) does not make a call wait:
it moves the chain on. An agent's run holds its place while its tools run,
so an agent with sub-agents on the same profile needs a place for itself and
one for each of them at a time.
@{bullet}A workdir whose gateway fixes values is not supported.
@{bullet}One process: the instance must run where the agent runs.
@{bullet}One more resume after an agy run that ended on a refused step: a call
that needs it takes as long as two runs, and the second sees the note, not
the step's refusal itself.
@{bullet}An agy run is a coding_cli run with a worktree and a branch of its
own, which coding_cli does not remove -- one per conversation, not per call:
its workdir is a small repository of its own; clear its worktrees now and
then (coding_cli guide). Claude Code's runs work in the instance's room and
leave none.
@{bullet}Images and other parts that are not text are named, not passed.
@{bullet}No streaming: the answer arrives when the run ends.
@endnode
